-- ------------------------------------------------------------ Udaipur
   '["Udaipur sits in a bowl of the Aravalli hills in southern Rajasthan. Maharana Udai Singh II founded it in 1559 as the new capital of the Mewar kingdom, ruled by the Sisodia Rajputs.","The city grew around a chain of man-made lakes, and its rulers built marble palaces right at the water''s edge. That is why travellers call it the City of Lakes, and sometimes the Venice of the East."]'::jsonb, '[["Founded","1559"],["Founder","Maharana Udai Singh II"],["Kingdom","Mewar"],["Language","Mewari, Hindi"]]'::jsonb, '[["Udaipur (founding, Mewar)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Udaipur"],["Sisodia dynasty","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sisodia_dynasty"],["Mewari language","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mewari_language"],["Khamma Ghani greeting","https://avtans.com/2026/03/17/khamma-ghani-origins-and-significance-explored/"],["Venice of the East","https://www.wildfrontierstravel.com/en_AU/blog/in-udaipur-venice-of-the-east"]]'::jsonb, 0);

-- ------------------------------------------------------------ Kolkata
   '["Kolkata stands on the east bank of the Hooghly river, a branch of the Ganga. It grew from three villages, Sutanuti, Gobindapur and Kalikata, where the East India Company set up a trading post in 1690.","It was the capital of British India until 1911 and the heart of the Bengal Renaissance, a burst of new ideas in literature, science and social reform. The city officially changed its name from Calcutta to Kolkata in 2001."]'::jsonb, '[["Trading post","1690"],["Capital of British India","until 1911"],["Renamed Kolkata","2001"],["Language","Bengali"]]'::jsonb, '[["Hooghly river (Britannica)","https://www.britannica.com/place/Hugli-River"],["The three villages and 1690","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalikata"],["Capital until 1911, renamed 2001","https://www.newworldencyclopedia.org/entry/kolkata"],["City of Joy nickname","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Joy"]]'::jsonb, 1);
